# Composed modules allowed to federate their REST prefix through the gateway,
# as comma-separated `prefix:sha256hex` entries. The digest is of that module's
# own registration secret, which a composition provisions into the module and
# declares here; only the digest lives on this side. Empty means no module may
# register.
#
# This group exists on its own so a composition can declare it — overriding a
# group replaces it — without having to restate the unrelated keys it would
# otherwise inherit from a shared group.
MODULE_REGISTRATION_SECRETS=

# Module service-principal identity digests, as comma-separated prefix:sha256hex
# entries, independent of route-registration credentials. Empty means no module
# may obtain a Work Context. Registration credentials never substitute for an
# absent identity declaration.
MODULE_IDENTITY_SECRETS=

# Solutions allowed to register their gateway upstream and their frontend
# Module-Federation remote, in the same `id:sha256hex` form. Declared separately
# from MODULE_REGISTRATION_SECRETS on purpose: a solution remote executes
# JavaScript in the host origin with the viewer's credentials, which is strictly
# more authority than federating a REST prefix. Empty means no solution may
# register.
#
# Unlike the module keys above, this one is read on every registration exchange:
# a solution mounts against a host that is already serving, so adding or removing
# an entry here takes effect without restarting accounts.
SOLUTION_REGISTRATION_SECRETS=

# Declared solution presence (issue #952). The directory delivery places rendered
# SolutionHostBinding documents in, and the coordinate this host answers for.
#
# Empty SOLUTION_HOST_BINDINGS_DIR leaves the reconciler off, which is the
# default while the runtimes still register themselves: nothing on this host is
# declared, and every solution is present because it heartbeats. Setting the
# mount WITHOUT the coordinate refuses to boot — a host that does not know its
# own coordinate cannot refuse a document delivered to another host, and that
# refusal is the only thing separating this host's desired state from a
# neighbour's.
#
# The coordinate is the exact string an operator declared on the environment the
# renderer read (`environments[].host.coordinate`). It is never derived here: a
# coordinate this host invented would match nothing delivery ever wrote, so every
# document would be refused as targeting a different host.
SOLUTION_HOST_BINDINGS_DIR=
SOLUTION_HOST_COORDINATE=

# The ownership domains this host accepts delivery from, comma-separated.
# Required with the mount, and empty refuses to boot.
#
# Core refuses a document whose ownership domain this host has not stated, and
# the reason is that the applied record cannot bound a binding's FIRST
# generation: there is nothing yet to compare a domain against, so without the
# declaration any delivery could claim an unseen binding ID under a domain of
# its own choosing and own it from then on. It is the host's to declare because
# Core cannot know which delivery is entitled to a name it has never seen.
SOLUTION_HOST_OWNERSHIP_DOMAINS=

# Which ownership domains each ATTESTED SIGNER IDENTITY may deliver under, as
# comma-separated `<identity>=<domain>[|<domain>...]` entries. Required with the
# mount, and empty refuses to boot.
#
# A delivered document asserts its own ownership domain, so the domain list above
# says what this host accepts at all and this says WHO may speak for each one.
# Without it, any signer this host attested could deliver under any domain it
# accepts and take over bindings in that domain. An entry naming a domain the
# list above does not accept is refused by name rather than ignored: a typo that
# silently grants nothing reads exactly like a policy that works, right up until
# the binding it was meant to admit is withheld.
#
# Identities are keyless certificate SANs, which contain `/`, `:` and `@` — so
# `,` separates entries and `|` separates domains.
SOLUTION_HOST_SIGNER_DOMAINS=

# How a delivered carrier's bundle is checked: `keyless` or `local`. Required
# with the mount, with NO DEFAULT, because every default is wrong somewhere —
# trusting delivery in production, or refusing everything on a laptop.
#
# `keyless` verifies a Sigstore bundle against a trust root and an identity
# allowlist, never a key. It is NOT YET AVAILABLE and refuses at boot: nothing
# here provisions the trust root or the allowlist, and both must be provisioned
# independently of delivery, because a deployer who can edit the allowlist need
# not forge a signature when it can replace the verifier.
#
# `local` performs NO cryptography and attests every carrier as the identity
# `local`, so it is usable only on a local coordinate (`local/…`, `localhost/…`)
# and that is enforced in code. Grant the `local` identity its domains in
# SOLUTION_HOST_SIGNER_DOMAINS above: the local policy goes through the
# signer-to-domain check, not around it.
SOLUTION_HOST_TRUST_POLICY=

# How often the host re-reads its mount. Delivery replaces a document at its own
# cadence and gives the host no signal, so the pass is a poll and this is the
# convergence bound. Empty uses the built-in default (30s).
SOLUTION_HOST_BINDING_INTERVAL=
